WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.849 --> 00:00:03.241
You connect the filling unit of line 3.

2
00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:06.584
An attack on the office must not stop that line.

3
00:00:07.581 --> 00:00:12.225
Cybersecurity teaches you what a plant must arrange and how to talk about it.

4
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.091
Four topics follow the attack.

5
00:00:15.806 --> 00:00:19.254
First how it gets in and how the line defends itself.

6
00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:23.874
Then what the law asks and what you do when it happens anyway.

7
00:00:25.134 --> 00:00:26.922
The first topic is Basics.

8
00:00:27.646 --> 00:00:31.071
In the office, confidentiality often comes first.

9
00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:35.393
In the plant, availability and safety come first.

10
00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:38.909
A fake mail is the most common way in.

11
00:00:39.612 --> 00:00:40.854
That is called phishing.

12
00:00:41.857 --> 00:00:45.967
With a report button you send a suspicious mail to IT in one click.

13
00:00:47.274 --> 00:00:53.416
The second topic is OT security with IEC 62443.

14
00:00:54.136 --> 00:00:57.038
A standard for plant, integrator and supplier.

15
00:00:58.083 --> 00:01:00.649
Line 3 gets a floor area of its own.

16
00:01:01.391 --> 00:01:05.617
One guarded link runs from the network cabinet to the control cabinet.

17
00:01:06.661 --> 00:01:10.353
So an infection from the office does not simply get onto the line.

18
00:01:11.324 --> 00:01:14.145
You lay out the plant with zones and conduits.

19
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:17.987
The third topic is NIS2 and the law.

20
00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:21.926
In the Netherlands that law is the Cybersecurity Act.

21
00:01:22.962 --> 00:01:27.386
The law has a duty of care, a reporting duty and a registration duty.

22
00:01:28.382 --> 00:01:31.598
An attack that stops line 3 may call for a report.

23
00:01:32.651 --> 00:01:36.157
Whether the plant is covered depends on sector and size.

24
00:01:37.216 --> 00:01:40.560
You look up your sector in Annex I and Annex II.

25
00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.323
The fourth topic is Incident response.

26
00:01:45.015 --> 00:01:47.732
That is the fixed way of handling an incident.

27
00:01:48.811 --> 00:01:53.815
First preparation and detection, then containment, recovery and lessons learned.

28
00:01:54.892 --> 00:01:58.909
In containment you might close the link between the office and the plant.

29
00:01:59.991 --> 00:02:04.495
For when the MES fails, you write a manual fallback procedure.

30
00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.962
So line 3 can keep running when the office is attacked.

31
00:02:09.786 --> 00:02:12.073
Then you move on to the line itself.

32
00:02:13.351 --> 00:02:14.140
The whole map.

33
00:02:14.869 --> 00:02:18.793
Cybersecurity is the third station of Automation and IT.

34
00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:21.497
Every topic has four levels.

35
00:02:22.504 --> 00:02:27.415
Start at Basics level with confidentiality, integrity and availability.

36
00:02:28.136 --> 00:02:29.529
That is what you protect.

37
00:02:30.584 --> 00:02:33.882
At Working knowledge level come phishing, dividing the plant,

38
00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:35.635
who is covered and preparing.

39
00:02:36.659 --> 00:02:40.444
At Advanced level it is about MFA, how strict to be,

40
00:02:40.653 --> 00:02:43.950
the duty of care in practice and containing an attack.

41
00:02:44.975 --> 00:02:48.922
At Expert level it is about least privilege, patching in OT,

42
00:02:49.085 --> 00:02:51.186
reporting incidents and recovering.

43
00:02:52.198 --> 00:02:56.343
Every lesson and every film in this module has a place on this map.
